{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Go down, for, etc.</b> He doubled the expression <b>Go down</b> — one for his person, that he should go, and one to tell him that he should descend from his greatness. And if He had said only רד, we would say that because he was on the mountain, the language of descent was used, as indeed He says (Exodus 19:14): ״וַיֵּרֶד מֹשֶׁה מִן הָהָר״. And we need to know what the nature of this descent is. Perhaps He informed him that all his greatness was on account of Israel, and from now on, since they sinned, he would not attain prophecy as at first. Or it may be that there, in the place where he stood, he no longer had the strength to remain, since the supporter had failed — and that is what He said to him, <b>Go down</b>, meaning: do not stand in the first place. And the reason — <b>for your people has corrupted</b> — is that through their strength you ascended." "And it is difficult: if so, He should have said this from the moment Israel said ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״, if it is because Israel committed idolatry that Moses' descent from his level is required. And one may further ask why HaShem needed to elaborate in informing the sin of Israel, especially according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 43a), who said regarding the incident of Achan that Joshua asked something of HaShem to inform him who had trespassed in the consecrated items, and HaShem's response to him was: ״I am not a informer to you״? If so, here in our passage He should not have told Moses the evil things that Israel did. And it is true that there it was a matter that was not destined to be revealed, and there is an element of informership in that, unlike this case, which was a matter destined to be revealed when Moses descended from the mountain. Nevertheless, relative to the source of piety and purity of tongue — choice silver — the tongue of a righteous one would not speak such things." "<b>However,</b> the reason for HaShem's words is that, Heaven forbid, it was not to inform upon one who does evil that Elohim came, but to justify His righteous judgment. That is, since He said to Moses <b>Go down</b> from your greatness, it was necessary to state for what reason the beloved's house was stricken, what his transgression was that Elohim should bring him down. And therefore He said <b>for your people has corrupted</b>, and it fell upon Him to inform him of the nature of the corruption for which Moses deserved the descent. And He arranged three things: (1) they made for themselves, etc.; (2) <b>and they bowed to it and sacrificed</b>, etc.; (3) <b>and they said, these</b>, etc. — and they correspond to thought, speech, and action. He began with thought and said <b>they made for themselves</b> — behold, thought: from the time they made it they intended idolatry, and this is what He precisely intimated by saying <b>for themselves</b>. And see what I wrote on the verse (Exodus 20:3) ״לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים״, etc. Corresponding to action He said <b>and they bowed to it and sacrificed to it</b> — there is no greater action than this. Corresponding to speech He said <b>and they said, these are your gods</b>, etc. And because of this, Moses' strength failed in three dimensions, which are nefesh, ruach, and neshamah, and he could no longer remain on the first level. And from this inference you know that before Israel transgressed in these three particulars, Moses was still able to remain in the mediating level without blemish — for if it were not so, and with only one or two of the aforementioned there would be reason for the descent, HaShem would not have revealed to Moses all the deeds because of the taint of informership, as we wrote. And from now on there is no place for our difficulty: why did he not descend from the moment they said ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ or from the moment they said to Aaron ״Make us gods״?"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "In comment [1], the Aramaic phrase דֵּלָטוֹר (delator, 'informer') is rendered literally as HaShem's response to Joshua; the word is left transliterated in the English to preserve the halakhic concept under discussion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:35:51.837453567Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}